    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "It's good that you're being careful, but those tiny white flakes were likely just dust, not a sign of an internal chocolate problem. Even if you're not seeing fat bloom, other environmental factors can cause other kinds of visual issues.\n\nBesides temperature, moisture is a major culprit, which can lead to \"sugar bloom\" if the chocolate is exposed to humidity. If the environment is too damp, the sugar on the surface dissolves and recrystallizes when the moisture evaporates, creating a gritty, white appearance. It's a different bloom than fat bloom, but it's equally undesirable. Also, rapid temperature fluctuations, like moving the chocolate from a hot kitchen to a cold storage area too quickly, can stress the cocoa butter and trigger instability.",
